1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,762
Your photo app can spot your cat.

2
00:00:02,762 --> 00:00:04,596
Yet nobody had to write down

3
00:00:04,596 --> 00:00:06,955
rule by rule, what a cat looks like.

4
00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:09,503
So how does it know? In short:

5
00:00:09,503 --> 00:00:11,554
you show it lots of examples

6
00:00:11,554 --> 00:00:14,191
and it works out the pattern itself.

7
00:00:14,191 --> 00:00:15,510
That's today's AI.

8
00:00:21,233 --> 00:00:22,481
Four things today.

9
00:00:22,831 --> 00:00:24,956
The problem AI is built to solve.

10
00:00:25,306 --> 00:00:27,221
A child learning their numbers

11
00:00:27,221 --> 00:00:28,626
and a real experiment.

12
00:00:28,976 --> 00:00:31,296
Seeing AI as a prediction machine.

13
00:00:31,646 --> 00:00:33,997
What it changes, what can go wrong

14
00:00:33,997 --> 00:00:36,003
and three things to remember.

15
00:00:36,353 --> 00:00:38,176
You don't need any background.

16
00:00:42,300 --> 00:00:44,880
Start with a traditional computer program.

17
00:00:44,880 --> 00:00:46,968
People write the rules, one by one

18
00:00:46,968 --> 00:00:48,811
and the computer follows them.

19
00:00:49,161 --> 00:00:51,168
For payroll, or booking a flight

20
00:00:51,168 --> 00:00:52,861
the rules are crystal clear

21
00:00:52,861 --> 00:00:54,115
and that works fine.

22
00:00:54,465 --> 00:00:55,986
But some tasks are hard

23
00:00:55,986 --> 00:00:57,308
to capture in rules.

24
00:00:57,308 --> 00:00:59,755
Like recognizing a handwritten three.

25
00:01:00,105 --> 00:01:02,106
Every one of these is a three

26
00:01:02,106 --> 00:01:03,210
written by hand.

27
00:01:03,210 --> 00:01:05,971
Some are fat, some are thin, some slant.

28
00:01:05,971 --> 00:01:08,110
You recognize them at a glance.

29
00:01:08,460 --> 00:01:10,003
But try explaining how.

30
00:01:10,003 --> 00:01:12,082
Two bumps, one above the other?

31
00:01:12,082 --> 00:01:13,625
An eight has those too.

32
00:01:13,625 --> 00:01:15,704
Open on the left at the bottom?

33
00:01:15,704 --> 00:01:16,576
So is a five.

34
00:01:16,926 --> 00:01:18,113
Rules like these can

35
00:01:18,113 --> 00:01:19,477
easily miss some threes

36
00:01:19,477 --> 00:01:21,554
or mistake other digits for threes.

37
00:01:23,067 --> 00:01:24,643
Most of today's AI takes

38
00:01:24,643 --> 00:01:26,021
a different approach.

39
00:01:26,021 --> 00:01:28,188
Don't write rules. Give examples.

40
00:01:28,538 --> 00:01:30,216
Show the computer hundreds

41
00:01:30,216 --> 00:01:32,603
even thousands, of handwritten digits

42
00:01:32,603 --> 00:01:34,217
each one with the answer:

43
00:01:34,217 --> 00:01:36,346
this is a three, this is a seven.

44
00:01:36,696 --> 00:01:37,831
It finds the pattern

45
00:01:37,831 --> 00:01:39,363
in the examples on its own.

46
00:01:39,363 --> 00:01:41,689
That approach is called machine learning.

47
00:01:42,039 --> 00:01:43,846
The way of telling digits apart

48
00:01:43,846 --> 00:01:46,118
that it ends up with is called a model.

49
00:01:46,118 --> 00:01:47,633
Hand the model a new image

50
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:49,265
and it gives you its answer.

51
00:01:49,615 --> 00:01:51,401
Many people think a programmer

52
00:01:51,401 --> 00:01:53,841
typed in everything an AI can do. In fact

53
00:01:53,841 --> 00:01:56,043
most of it was learned from examples.

54
00:01:56,043 --> 00:01:57,888
Those examples are called data.

55
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,676
It's like teaching a child their numbers.

56
00:02:04,676 --> 00:02:07,221
You don't hand them a manual that says:

57
00:02:07,221 --> 00:02:09,375
a 3 is two half circles, stacked.

58
00:02:09,725 --> 00:02:11,481
You just point and keep saying

59
00:02:11,481 --> 00:02:13,882
"This is a three." Eventually they get it

60
00:02:13,882 --> 00:02:15,638
even when the three is written

61
00:02:15,638 --> 00:02:17,277
in a way they've never seen.

62
00:02:17,627 --> 00:02:19,529
The way of telling numbers apart

63
00:02:19,529 --> 00:02:21,727
that the child picks up is the model.

64
00:02:21,727 --> 00:02:23,094
Each digit you point at

65
00:02:23,094 --> 00:02:24,758
plus your "this is a three,"

66
00:02:24,758 --> 00:02:26,541
is an example with its answer.

67
00:02:26,891 --> 00:02:28,727
Two things don't match, though.

68
00:02:28,727 --> 00:02:31,154
First, a model leans on lots of examples:

69
00:02:31,154 --> 00:02:31,924
as you'll see

70
00:02:31,924 --> 00:02:33,108
ours does far better

71
00:02:33,108 --> 00:02:34,825
with a hundred than with ten.

72
00:02:34,825 --> 00:02:36,661
And a child comes to understand

73
00:02:36,661 --> 00:02:38,437
that three means three things.

74
00:02:38,437 --> 00:02:40,450
The model has only learned to look

75
00:02:40,450 --> 00:02:42,286
at an image and pick an answer.

76
00:02:42,636 --> 00:02:44,141
Or think about cooking.

77
00:02:44,141 --> 00:02:46,367
Sometimes you just follow a recipe

78
00:02:46,367 --> 00:02:47,218
step by step.

79
00:02:47,218 --> 00:02:49,116
That's a traditional program.

80
00:02:49,116 --> 00:02:51,800
Other times, a seasoned cook goes by feel

81
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:54,353
built over a thousand dishes of tasting

82
00:02:54,353 --> 00:02:57,429
getting feedback, and adjusting whatever's off.

83
00:02:57,779 --> 00:02:58,736
Each dish is like

84
00:02:58,736 --> 00:03:00,143
the model making a guess.

85
00:03:00,143 --> 00:03:02,395
Tasting it and hearing what people think

86
00:03:02,395 --> 00:03:04,478
is checking against the right answer.

87
00:03:04,478 --> 00:03:06,787
Adjusting when it's off: that's learning.

88
00:03:06,787 --> 00:03:09,151
And the feel they end up with is something

89
00:03:09,151 --> 00:03:11,234
even the cook can't fully write down.

90
00:03:15,367 --> 00:03:17,093
Let's actually try it.

91
00:03:17,093 --> 00:03:19,133
Here's a public collection

92
00:03:19,133 --> 00:03:21,878
of handwritten digits: 1,797 images

93
00:03:21,878 --> 00:03:24,467
each just an eight-by-eight grid.

94
00:03:24,817 --> 00:03:26,747
First, we lock away three hundred

95
00:03:26,747 --> 00:03:27,858
of them as the exam

96
00:03:27,858 --> 00:03:30,256
the rest are the examples it learns from.

97
00:03:30,256 --> 00:03:32,654
Learning from examples is called training

98
00:03:32,654 --> 00:03:34,350
and during training the model

99
00:03:34,350 --> 00:03:35,520
never sees the exam.

100
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:36,806
Why split it this way?

101
00:03:36,806 --> 00:03:38,912
Only questions it has never seen can

102
00:03:38,912 --> 00:03:40,842
tell us whether it really learned

103
00:03:40,842 --> 00:03:41,953
or just remembered.

104
00:03:42,303 --> 00:03:43,075
Take a guess:

105
00:03:43,075 --> 00:03:44,382
with just ten examples

106
00:03:44,382 --> 00:03:46,759
how much of the exam would it get right?

107
00:03:48,609 --> 00:03:49,828
Pick ten at random

108
00:03:49,828 --> 00:03:51,452
and usually a few digits

109
00:03:51,452 --> 00:03:52,874
don't show up at all.

110
00:03:52,874 --> 00:03:54,160
Try it twenty times

111
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:56,530
and the typical score is about 37%.

112
00:03:56,530 --> 00:03:58,764
That beats random guessing at 10%

113
00:03:58,764 --> 00:04:00,795
but it's still a long way off.

114
00:04:01,145 --> 00:04:04,224
With a hundred examples: 88%.

115
00:04:04,574 --> 00:04:06,697
With all of the nearly fifteen

116
00:04:06,697 --> 00:04:08,890
hundred training examples: 97%.

117
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:11,030
At no point did anyone tell

118
00:04:11,030 --> 00:04:12,754
it what a three looks like

119
00:04:12,754 --> 00:04:14,742
only which images were threes.

120
00:04:14,742 --> 00:04:15,936
In this experiment

121
00:04:15,936 --> 00:04:18,190
more examples meant higher scores.

122
00:04:18,190 --> 00:04:19,516
Your photo app finds

123
00:04:19,516 --> 00:04:20,974
your cat the same way.

124
00:04:21,324 --> 00:04:24,041
Still, out of three hundred exam questions

125
00:04:24,041 --> 00:04:24,947
it missed ten.

126
00:04:24,947 --> 00:04:26,177
Like this messy two

127
00:04:26,177 --> 00:04:27,665
which it read as a one.

128
00:04:27,665 --> 00:04:29,218
A few of them would make

129
00:04:29,218 --> 00:04:30,641
a person hesitate too.

130
00:04:34,767 --> 00:04:36,509
Here's another way to see it.

131
00:04:36,509 --> 00:04:38,913
Today's AI is like a prediction machine.

132
00:04:38,913 --> 00:04:40,475
Not predicting the future:

133
00:04:40,475 --> 00:04:41,737
predicting the answer

134
00:04:41,737 --> 00:04:43,239
for whatever you show it.

135
00:04:43,589 --> 00:04:45,840
Our model doesn't just guess.

136
00:04:45,840 --> 00:04:48,247
It also reports how sure it is.

137
00:04:48,247 --> 00:04:49,412
For this image:

138
00:04:49,412 --> 00:04:51,275
"96% sure it's a three."

139
00:04:51,625 --> 00:04:53,440
Other AI works the same way.

140
00:04:53,440 --> 00:04:55,385
An image model guesses whether

141
00:04:55,385 --> 00:04:56,941
a photo has a cat in it.

142
00:04:56,941 --> 00:04:58,367
A speech model guesses

143
00:04:58,367 --> 00:04:59,793
which words were said.

144
00:05:00,143 --> 00:05:02,652
Give a language model half a sentence

145
00:05:02,652 --> 00:05:04,619
and it guesses the next word.

146
00:05:04,619 --> 00:05:06,451
Keep going, word after word

147
00:05:06,451 --> 00:05:08,214
and you get a whole reply.

148
00:05:08,564 --> 00:05:09,829
Large language models

149
00:05:09,829 --> 00:05:11,635
the technology behind chatbots

150
00:05:11,635 --> 00:05:13,382
write their replies this way.

151
00:05:13,382 --> 00:05:15,430
They're extremely good at guessing

152
00:05:15,430 --> 00:05:16,755
and they pick up a lot

153
00:05:16,755 --> 00:05:18,140
of knowledge from data.

154
00:05:18,140 --> 00:05:19,585
But a smooth reply isn't

155
00:05:19,585 --> 00:05:21,151
a guarantee of a true one.

156
00:05:22,667 --> 00:05:25,001
These terms nest, circle within circle.

157
00:05:25,001 --> 00:05:28,113
The outermost circle is AI, artificial intelligence:

158
00:05:28,113 --> 00:05:29,909
getting computers to do things

159
00:05:29,909 --> 00:05:31,764
that seem to take intelligence.

160
00:05:31,764 --> 00:05:33,679
A chess program built from rules

161
00:05:33,679 --> 00:05:35,235
people wrote counts as AI.

162
00:05:35,235 --> 00:05:37,390
An ordinary payroll program doesn't.

163
00:05:37,740 --> 00:05:39,842
Machine learning sits inside AI.

164
00:05:39,842 --> 00:05:41,747
Inside that is deep learning.

165
00:05:41,747 --> 00:05:44,178
It uses models called neural networks

166
00:05:44,178 --> 00:05:45,623
built from many layers

167
00:05:45,623 --> 00:05:47,134
of simple calculations.

168
00:05:47,134 --> 00:05:49,040
Don't let the names fool you:

169
00:05:49,040 --> 00:05:51,076
these networks are only loosely

170
00:05:51,076 --> 00:05:52,587
like neurons in a brain

171
00:05:52,587 --> 00:05:54,755
and "deep" just means many layers

172
00:05:54,755 --> 00:05:56,266
not deep understanding.

173
00:05:56,616 --> 00:05:57,923
Large language models

174
00:05:57,923 --> 00:05:59,728
are built with deep learning.

175
00:05:59,728 --> 00:06:00,661
In this episode

176
00:06:00,661 --> 00:06:01,781
"today's AI" means

177
00:06:01,781 --> 00:06:03,524
the machine learning circle.

178
00:06:07,633 --> 00:06:09,464
This ability to learn from data

179
00:06:09,464 --> 00:06:11,531
is already changing the real world.

180
00:06:11,881 --> 00:06:13,272
Finding a protein's shape

181
00:06:13,272 --> 00:06:14,885
often took years of lab work.

182
00:06:14,885 --> 00:06:16,442
AlphaFold learned from known

183
00:06:16,442 --> 00:06:18,556
structures to predict shapes directly.

184
00:06:18,906 --> 00:06:20,653
Its two lead developers went

185
00:06:20,653 --> 00:06:22,399
on to share half of the 2024

186
00:06:22,399 --> 00:06:24,395
Nobel Prize in Chemistry for it.

187
00:06:24,745 --> 00:06:26,779
About two months after launch

188
00:06:26,779 --> 00:06:29,024
ChatGPT had an estimated hundred

189
00:06:29,024 --> 00:06:31,058
million monthly active users.

190
00:06:31,408 --> 00:06:34,090
But AI can also learn the wrong lessons.

191
00:06:34,090 --> 00:06:35,432
According to Reuters

192
00:06:35,432 --> 00:06:36,974
Amazon once tried using

193
00:06:36,974 --> 00:06:38,382
AI to screen résumés.

194
00:06:38,382 --> 00:06:40,394
That AI learned from ten years

195
00:06:40,394 --> 00:06:42,406
of résumés sent to the company

196
00:06:42,406 --> 00:06:43,479
mostly from men.

197
00:06:43,829 --> 00:06:45,910
In effect, it taught itself that male

198
00:06:45,910 --> 00:06:47,429
candidates were preferable.

199
00:06:47,429 --> 00:06:48,667
It marked down résumés

200
00:06:48,667 --> 00:06:50,017
with the word "women's,"

201
00:06:50,017 --> 00:06:51,986
as in "women's chess club captain."

202
00:06:51,986 --> 00:06:53,561
The tool was later scrapped.

203
00:06:53,911 --> 00:06:55,423
Bias in the data can be learned

204
00:06:55,423 --> 00:06:57,032
right along with everything else.

205
00:07:01,133 --> 00:07:02,185
So when you use AI

206
00:07:02,185 --> 00:07:04,407
here are three things to keep in mind.

207
00:07:04,757 --> 00:07:07,445
One: unfamiliar inputs can fool it.

208
00:07:07,445 --> 00:07:10,286
We showed this digit-only model an A.

209
00:07:10,286 --> 00:07:13,742
It had to pick from zero to nine. Its answer?

210
00:07:13,742 --> 00:07:15,508
"92% sure it's a four."

211
00:07:15,858 --> 00:07:17,538
That confidence only compares

212
00:07:17,538 --> 00:07:19,508
the ten digits against each other.

213
00:07:19,508 --> 00:07:20,493
A child would say

214
00:07:20,493 --> 00:07:22,753
"That's not a number." The model can't.

215
00:07:23,103 --> 00:07:25,836
Two: it gives predictions, not truth.

216
00:07:25,836 --> 00:07:27,608
Remember that messy two?

217
00:07:27,608 --> 00:07:29,676
It was 81% sure it was a one

218
00:07:29,676 --> 00:07:30,932
and it was wrong.

219
00:07:31,282 --> 00:07:33,420
Three: its strengths and flaws come

220
00:07:33,420 --> 00:07:35,192
from the data it learned from

221
00:07:35,192 --> 00:07:36,230
and also from how

222
00:07:36,230 --> 00:07:37,941
it was designed and trained.

223
00:07:37,941 --> 00:07:40,201
To judge whether an AI is trustworthy

224
00:07:40,201 --> 00:07:42,400
first ask what data it learned from.

225
00:07:42,750 --> 00:07:44,176
Next time an AI answers

226
00:07:44,176 --> 00:07:46,655
you with total confidence, ask yourself:

227
00:07:46,655 --> 00:07:49,258
has it ever learned this kind of question?

228
00:07:50,767 --> 00:07:51,719
Remember this:

229
00:07:52,069 --> 00:07:53,963
Today's AI isn't mainly built

230
00:07:53,963 --> 00:07:55,531
from rules people write.

231
00:07:55,881 --> 00:07:58,361
It teaches itself patterns from examples.

232
00:07:58,711 --> 00:08:00,200
Next time: overfitting.

233
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:01,430
Why can a model ace

234
00:08:01,430 --> 00:08:02,854
its practice questions

235
00:08:02,854 --> 00:08:04,407
and still fail new ones?
